1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:01,590
As you get more users,

2
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:04,200
your database grows and your tables get

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,213
bigger. Now it might take a little bit longer

4
00:00:06,213 --> 00:00:07,993
for your app to load and you're now having

5
00:00:07,993 --> 00:00:09,033
performance issues.

6
00:00:09,033 --> 00:00:10,943
One thing that you can always use to fix

7
00:00:10,943 --> 00:00:12,393
this is a database index.

8
00:00:12,393 --> 00:00:13,203
In this video,

9
00:00:13,203 --> 00:00:14,933
we'll go over what your

10
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:16,893
database does when there is no index,

11
00:00:16,893 --> 00:00:18,103
what an index actually is,

12
00:00:18,103 --> 00:00:20,673
how it helps find things so fast and helps

13
00:00:20,673 --> 00:00:21,313
performance,

14
00:00:21,313 --> 00:00:23,323
and the different types of indexes,

15
00:00:23,323 --> 00:00:25,713
the mistakes that people make when they use

16
00:00:25,713 --> 00:00:26,583
database indexes.

17
00:00:26,583 --> 00:00:27,363
To understand

18
00:00:27,363 --> 00:00:28,863
why a database index matters,

19
00:00:28,863 --> 00:00:30,463
especially for performance,

20
00:00:30,463 --> 00:00:32,093
you first have to understand

21
00:00:32,093 --> 00:00:34,283
what your database does without an index.

22
00:00:34,283 --> 00:00:35,376
Say we have a bookstore,

23
00:00:35,376 --> 00:00:38,093
and the books table in our database has a

24
00:00:38,093 --> 00:00:38,973
million rows,

25
00:00:38,973 --> 00:00:40,633
we have a million books

26
00:00:40,633 --> 00:00:41,343
in there,

27
00:00:41,343 --> 00:00:42,363
and we run this query.

28
00:00:42,363 --> 00:00:43,443
Without an index,

29
00:00:43,443 --> 00:00:46,053
the database has no idea where author 42's

30
00:00:46,053 --> 00:00:46,753
books are,

31
00:00:46,753 --> 00:00:48,713
so it does the only thing it can do.

32
00:00:48,713 --> 00:00:50,853
It starts at the first row and it checks

33
00:00:50,853 --> 00:00:51,823
every single row,

34
00:00:51,823 --> 00:00:53,773
all one million of them,

35
00:00:53,773 --> 00:00:56,293
and that is called a full table scan.

36
00:00:56,293 --> 00:00:57,403
And in many cases,

37
00:00:57,403 --> 00:00:59,243
it can be very problematic.

38
00:00:59,243 --> 00:01:00,935
When your table has maybe 10,

39
00:01:00,935 --> 00:01:01,523
100 rows,

40
00:01:01,523 --> 00:01:03,153
maybe it wouldn't be a problem,

41
00:01:03,153 --> 00:01:04,843
maybe you wouldn't notice,

42
00:01:04,843 --> 00:01:06,063
but at a million rows,

43
00:01:06,063 --> 00:01:07,363
then it's a problem.

44
00:01:07,363 --> 00:01:09,483
What is a database index and how does it

45
00:01:09,483 --> 00:01:12,103
solve this problem of speed and performance?

46
00:01:12,103 --> 00:01:13,993
The easiest way to think about it is the

47
00:01:13,993 --> 00:01:15,803
index at the back of a book.

48
00:01:15,803 --> 00:01:17,363
If you want to find every page that mentions

49
00:01:17,363 --> 00:01:18,233
a particular word,

50
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:19,473
you don't read the whole book from the

51
00:01:19,473 --> 00:01:21,443
beginning. You go to the index at the back,

52
00:01:21,443 --> 00:01:22,773
it's sorted alphabetically,

53
00:01:22,773 --> 00:01:23,733
you find the word,

54
00:01:23,733 --> 00:01:25,533
and it tells you the page number.

55
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:27,803
A database index works the same way.

56
00:01:27,803 --> 00:01:29,233
It's a separate structure

57
00:01:29,233 --> 00:01:31,893
that stores the values from a column in sorted

58
00:01:31,893 --> 00:01:33,893
order, and for each value,

59
00:01:33,893 --> 00:01:35,033
it keeps a pointer

60
00:01:35,033 --> 00:01:36,583
to where that row actually lives.

61
00:01:36,583 --> 00:01:38,703
And it only stores the column and the pointer,

62
00:01:38,703 --> 00:01:41,303
so it's way much more smaller than the table

63
00:01:41,303 --> 00:01:44,213
itself. Now what mechanism does a database

64
00:01:44,213 --> 00:01:45,853
index use to find things so fast?

65
00:01:45,853 --> 00:01:47,093
I read an article on this,

66
00:01:47,093 --> 00:01:49,263
and I love the way the writer explained it,

67
00:01:49,263 --> 00:01:50,343
and I'm going to use the example.

68
00:01:50,343 --> 00:01:52,143
I will link the article in the description box

69
00:01:52,143 --> 00:01:52,503
as well.

70
00:01:52,503 --> 00:01:54,613
Say you have an index on the email column,

71
00:01:54,613 --> 00:01:56,713
and you're looking for bob@email.com.

72
00:01:56,713 --> 00:01:59,233
The database opens the index in the middle,

73
00:01:59,233 --> 00:02:01,043
and maybe sees dave@email.com.

74
00:02:01,523 --> 00:02:03,143
Bob comes before dave,

75
00:02:03,143 --> 00:02:05,033
so it moves to the left.

76
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:07,553
Now it sees alice@email.com.

77
00:02:07,553 --> 00:02:08,823
Bob comes after Alice,

78
00:02:08,823 --> 00:02:10,133
so it moves to the right,

79
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:11,413
and it keeps moving

80
00:02:11,413 --> 00:02:12,113
strategically

81
00:02:12,113 --> 00:02:14,253
like this until it finds bob@email.com

82
00:02:14,253 --> 00:02:15,253
in the index.

83
00:02:15,253 --> 00:02:17,503
Bob's entry in the index has a pointer

84
00:02:17,503 --> 00:02:19,913
to where Bob's row is in the table.

85
00:02:19,913 --> 00:02:21,533
So with this strategy,

86
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:23,453
even with a million rows,

87
00:02:23,453 --> 00:02:25,413
you can get to the row you're looking for

88
00:02:25,413 --> 00:02:27,213
in about 20 steps or so.

89
00:02:27,213 --> 00:02:28,533
Without an index,

90
00:02:28,533 --> 00:02:30,296
you will do up to a million checks in

91
00:02:30,296 --> 00:02:31,773
the worst case scenario.

92
00:02:31,773 --> 00:02:33,913
Most databases store their indexes in a

93
00:02:33,913 --> 00:02:34,723
structure

94
00:02:34,723 --> 00:02:35,657
called a B-tree,

95
00:02:35,657 --> 00:02:38,183
and a B-tree works the way that the example

96
00:02:38,183 --> 00:02:40,303
from the article showed.

97
00:02:40,303 --> 00:02:43,483
So even with a table with millions of rows,

98
00:02:43,483 --> 00:02:46,273
you usually only need a few levels to get

99
00:02:46,273 --> 00:02:47,853
to the right place.

100
00:02:47,853 --> 00:02:50,522
Adding a database index will literally up your

101
00:02:50,522 --> 00:02:51,673
query optimisation game,

102
00:02:51,673 --> 00:02:53,233
and it's kind of a one-liner.

103
00:02:53,233 --> 00:02:53,773
In SQL,

104
00:02:53,773 --> 00:02:55,073
it looks something like this.

105
00:02:55,073 --> 00:02:56,350
If you are using Laravel,

106
00:02:56,350 --> 00:02:58,370
example, you simply add it in your migration

107
00:02:58,370 --> 00:02:59,250
like this.

108
00:02:59,250 --> 00:03:00,950
Now when you search books by author,

109
00:03:00,950 --> 00:03:03,220
the database goes to the index first,

110
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:03,910
finds author 42,

111
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:05,760
and goes straight to those rows.

112
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:07,890
Once you've added your database index,

113
00:03:07,890 --> 00:03:10,080
you can confirm whether your query is actually

114
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:10,660
using it or not.

115
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:11,890
You can ask the database.

116
00:03:11,890 --> 00:03:13,930
Just put the word EXPLAIN in front of your

117
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:14,510
query.

118
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:15,287
In PostgreSQL,

119
00:03:15,287 --> 00:03:17,230
you will see Seq Scan.

120
00:03:17,230 --> 00:03:20,000
It means that the database is reading every

121
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:20,730
single row,

122
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:22,130
and if you see Index Scan,

123
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:24,290
it means that the database is using your

124
00:03:24,290 --> 00:03:25,845
index. Seq Scan,

125
00:03:25,845 --> 00:03:27,383
Sequential scan.

126
00:03:27,383 --> 00:03:28,033
In MySQL,

127
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:31,170
the results will come back in a small table,

128
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:32,750
so there are two columns to look at.

129
00:03:32,750 --> 00:03:33,550
If it says ALL,

130
00:03:33,550 --> 00:03:35,860
that means it's doing a full table scan.

131
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:37,460
If it says something like ref or range,

132
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:38,610
then it's using an index.

133
00:03:38,610 --> 00:03:40,000
So if your query is slow,

134
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,550
and you first want to confirm what is going

135
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:42,340
on, you can use,

136
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:43,660
and that confirms to you,

137
00:03:43,660 --> 00:03:45,230
is this query doing a full scan,

138
00:03:45,230 --> 00:03:46,650
or is it using an index?

139
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:48,570
There are a few types of database index.

140
00:03:48,570 --> 00:03:50,408
You hear people talk about B-tree,

141
00:03:50,408 --> 00:03:51,330
hash, bitmap,

142
00:03:51,330 --> 00:03:51,680
and many,

143
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:53,240
many like that.

144
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:54,000
I'm just

145
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,187
only going to focus on three in video.

146
00:03:56,187 --> 00:03:57,987
The first one is B-tree.

147
00:03:57,987 --> 00:04:00,977
B-tree. The B-tree is the default one,

148
00:04:00,977 --> 00:04:02,957
so when you create an index without saying

149
00:04:02,957 --> 00:04:03,727
what type it is,

150
00:04:03,727 --> 00:04:05,137
this is the one that you get.

151
00:04:05,137 --> 00:04:06,157
So it's very common.

152
00:04:06,157 --> 00:04:07,777
It can find exact matches,

153
00:04:07,777 --> 00:04:09,677
like the user with this email.

154
00:04:09,677 --> 00:04:10,837
It can find ranges,

155
00:04:10,837 --> 00:04:11,477
like

156
00:04:11,477 --> 00:04:13,457
books priced between 10 and 15.

157
00:04:13,457 --> 00:04:14,107
It can sort,

158
00:04:14,107 --> 00:04:15,307
like showing the newest

159
00:04:15,307 --> 00:04:16,037
order first,

160
00:04:16,037 --> 00:04:18,247
and it can find values by how they start,

161
00:04:18,247 --> 00:04:20,217
like every book title that starts with Harry.

162
00:04:20,217 --> 00:04:22,257
And that works because the index is sorted,

163
00:04:22,257 --> 00:04:24,340
so all the titles that start with Harry sit

164
00:04:24,340 --> 00:04:25,447
next to each other.

165
00:04:25,447 --> 00:04:28,487
The second commonly used type of database

166
00:04:28,487 --> 00:04:29,647
index is the unique index.

167
00:04:29,647 --> 00:04:31,387
It makes sure that no two rows have the

168
00:04:31,387 --> 00:04:32,097
same value.

169
00:04:32,097 --> 00:04:32,887
For example,

170
00:04:32,887 --> 00:04:34,697
no two user can sign up with the same

171
00:04:34,697 --> 00:04:37,657
email. Your primary key already has one.

172
00:04:37,657 --> 00:04:38,407
And the third one,

173
00:04:38,407 --> 00:04:39,957
this one kind of always confuses me.

174
00:04:39,957 --> 00:04:41,877
The third one is the composite index,

175
00:04:41,877 --> 00:04:43,067
which is an

176
00:04:43,067 --> 00:04:44,547
index on more than one column.

177
00:04:44,547 --> 00:04:46,815
So say you index last name and first name

178
00:04:46,815 --> 00:04:48,467
together in that order.

179
00:04:48,467 --> 00:04:50,217
It helps you when you search by last name,

180
00:04:50,217 --> 00:04:52,037
and it helps you when you search by last

181
00:04:52,037 --> 00:04:52,847
name and first name.

182
00:04:52,847 --> 00:04:54,407
But when you search by only first name,

183
00:04:54,407 --> 00:04:55,487
it usually doesn't help.

184
00:04:55,487 --> 00:04:56,657
A little bit of a reason,

185
00:04:56,657 --> 00:04:58,127
let's use the example with phone books to

186
00:04:58,127 --> 00:04:58,807
understand it.

187
00:04:58,807 --> 00:05:00,807
Think of a phone book sorted by surname.

188
00:05:00,807 --> 00:05:03,033
All the Adeyemis are together,

189
00:05:03,033 --> 00:05:05,423
all the Okafors are together.

190
00:05:05,423 --> 00:05:07,223
And inside each surname,

191
00:05:07,223 --> 00:05:09,383
it is sorted by first name.

192
00:05:09,383 --> 00:05:10,678
So in the Okafors,

193
00:05:10,678 --> 00:05:13,183
Ada Okafor will come before Chidi Okafor.

194
00:05:13,183 --> 00:05:14,933
So if I'm looking for Ada Okafor,

195
00:05:14,933 --> 00:05:15,593
it's easy.

196
00:05:15,593 --> 00:05:16,523
I go to the O's,

197
00:05:16,523 --> 00:05:17,251
I find Okafor,

198
00:05:17,251 --> 00:05:18,743
and then I find Ada.

199
00:05:18,743 --> 00:05:20,728
But what if I want everyone whose first name

200
00:05:20,728 --> 00:05:21,473
is Ada?

201
00:05:21,473 --> 00:05:23,779
There's probably Ada Adeyemi in the front,

202
00:05:23,779 --> 00:05:25,755
and Ada Okafor in the middle,

203
00:05:25,755 --> 00:05:27,773
and Ada Williams near the end.

204
00:05:27,773 --> 00:05:29,843
They are spread across the whole phone book

205
00:05:29,843 --> 00:05:32,333
because the book is sorted by surname first.

206
00:05:32,333 --> 00:05:34,373
So nothing groups the Adas together,

207
00:05:34,373 --> 00:05:35,893
and I would have to read every page to

208
00:05:35,893 --> 00:05:36,443
find them.

209
00:05:36,443 --> 00:05:38,693
So that's exactly what happens with the

210
00:05:38,693 --> 00:05:41,377
composite index on last name,

211
00:05:41,377 --> 00:05:42,317
first

212
00:05:42,367 --> 00:05:45,337
name. So the tip is to put the column

213
00:05:45,337 --> 00:05:47,907
you search by most often first.

214
00:05:47,907 --> 00:05:48,797
And for the rest,

215
00:05:48,797 --> 00:05:50,787
there are different kinds of searches.

216
00:05:50,787 --> 00:05:53,410
For the rest of the database index types,

217
00:05:53,410 --> 00:05:54,750
we have the hash index,

218
00:05:54,750 --> 00:05:56,995
which is for exact matches only,

219
00:05:56,995 --> 00:05:58,667
you know,

220
00:05:58,667 --> 00:06:01,027
like looking up someone's login session by its

221
00:06:01,027 --> 00:06:01,837
exact token,

222
00:06:01,837 --> 00:06:03,457
it cannot do range or

223
00:06:03,457 --> 00:06:05,707
sorting. So most people just use B-tree.

224
00:06:05,707 --> 00:06:07,777
A bitmap is for columns with only a few

225
00:06:07,777 --> 00:06:09,647
values, like a status column,

226
00:06:09,647 --> 00:06:11,627
that's only active or inactive or banned.

227
00:06:11,627 --> 00:06:13,597
You mostly see it in databases that are built

228
00:06:13,597 --> 00:06:14,647
for reports,

229
00:06:14,647 --> 00:06:16,103
yeah. And then we have spatial index,

230
00:06:16,103 --> 00:06:17,847
that one is mostly for location data,

231
00:06:17,847 --> 00:06:19,354
like when you search a restaurant name,

232
00:06:19,354 --> 00:06:20,793
a coffee shop near me,

233
00:06:20,793 --> 00:06:23,063
or when a ride app looks for drivers close

234
00:06:23,063 --> 00:06:23,863
to you.

235
00:06:23,863 --> 00:06:24,873
So it's groups,

236
00:06:24,873 --> 00:06:28,163
places that are near each other on the map,

237
00:06:28,163 --> 00:06:30,603
so the app only checks places around you,

238
00:06:30,603 --> 00:06:32,593
instead of every coffee shop or every

239
00:06:32,593 --> 00:06:33,863
restaurant in the world.

240
00:06:33,863 --> 00:06:35,773
And then we have the full-text index as well.

241
00:06:35,773 --> 00:06:37,553
It is for searching inside text.

242
00:06:37,553 --> 00:06:38,293
For example,

243
00:06:38,293 --> 00:06:40,563
when you search an online store for wireless

244
00:06:40,563 --> 00:06:42,983
headphones, it finds every product description

245
00:06:42,983 --> 00:06:44,453
with those words in right?

246
00:06:44,453 --> 00:06:46,509
So it works by indexing each word in the

247
00:06:46,509 --> 00:06:47,883
text instead of the whole value.

248
00:06:47,883 --> 00:06:50,543
And there's also covering index and partial

249
00:06:50,543 --> 00:06:53,483
index. You also hear about clustered and

250
00:06:53,483 --> 00:06:54,853
non-clustered

251
00:06:54,853 --> 00:06:56,833
indexes. And the difference between those is

252
00:06:56,833 --> 00:06:57,763
very simple.

253
00:06:57,763 --> 00:07:00,225
The clustered index decides the order the

254
00:07:00,225 --> 00:07:01,993
table's rows are actually stored.

255
00:07:01,993 --> 00:07:03,683
Think of this as a phone book again,

256
00:07:03,683 --> 00:07:05,139
the data itself is sorted by surname.

257
00:07:05,139 --> 00:07:06,532
So once you find the name,

258
00:07:06,532 --> 00:07:07,229
you find everything.

259
00:07:07,229 --> 00:07:09,163
A table can only have one clustered index

260
00:07:09,163 --> 00:07:11,260
because the rows can only be stored in one

261
00:07:11,260 --> 00:07:12,783
order. A non-clustered

262
00:07:12,783 --> 00:07:14,603
index is a separate structure

263
00:07:14,603 --> 00:07:15,913
that points back to the row.

264
00:07:15,913 --> 00:07:17,420
Think that as the index at the back of

265
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:17,910
the book,

266
00:07:17,910 --> 00:07:20,010
it tells you where to go and go there.

267
00:07:20,010 --> 00:07:22,470
So a table can have lots of non-clustered

268
00:07:22,470 --> 00:07:23,210
indexes.

269
00:07:23,210 --> 00:07:25,790
So this is the difference between clustered

270
00:07:25,790 --> 00:07:27,220
and non-clustered indexes.

271
00:07:27,220 --> 00:07:28,790
So now that begs the question,

272
00:07:28,790 --> 00:07:30,360
if database indexes

273
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:31,750
makes queries so fast,

274
00:07:31,750 --> 00:07:34,410
why don't we just index every column and be

275
00:07:34,410 --> 00:07:35,520
done with it?

276
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:36,520
We cannot,

277
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:38,061
because every index has a cost.

278
00:07:38,061 --> 00:07:39,150
Every time you insert,

279
00:07:39,150 --> 00:07:40,530
update and delete a row,

280
00:07:40,530 --> 00:07:41,180
the

281
00:07:41,180 --> 00:07:43,200
database also has to update every index on

282
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:44,040
that table.

283
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:46,500
So if your orders table has six indexes,

284
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:48,590
every new order means updating the whole table

285
00:07:48,590 --> 00:07:49,870
and all six indexes.

286
00:07:49,870 --> 00:07:51,980
So the more database indexes you have,

287
00:07:51,980 --> 00:07:53,800
the slower your writes get.

288
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:55,690
also indexes also take up storage

289
00:07:55,690 --> 00:07:58,190
space. And for a small table,

290
00:07:58,190 --> 00:08:00,340
the database will often skip your index and

291
00:08:00,340 --> 00:08:01,720
just scan the table anyway,

292
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:03,580
because that's faster for a small table,

293
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:04,940
you have just like 10 rows,

294
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:06,830
it's faster to read them than to go through

295
00:08:06,830 --> 00:08:07,920
an index first.

296
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:10,110
So what you do is that you only index

297
00:08:10,110 --> 00:08:12,900
the columns that you actually search on or

298
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:14,450
filter on or join or sort by,

299
00:08:14,450 --> 00:08:15,950
you don't index everything.

300
00:08:15,950 --> 00:08:17,257
But because as good as it is,

301
00:08:17,257 --> 00:08:18,717
it can also get costly too.

302
00:08:18,717 --> 00:08:20,707
And there are several mistakes you can make

303
00:08:20,707 --> 00:08:22,947
that can stop your database index from

304
00:08:22,947 --> 00:08:23,607
working as well.

305
00:08:23,607 --> 00:08:24,987
You can have a database

306
00:08:24,987 --> 00:08:27,793
index and the database still won't use it.

307
00:08:27,793 --> 00:08:29,763
And these are the common mistakes that cause

308
00:08:29,763 --> 00:08:31,816
it. The first one is wrapping your column in

309
00:08:31,816 --> 00:08:32,433
a function.

310
00:08:32,433 --> 00:08:33,303
For example,

311
00:08:33,303 --> 00:08:34,133
in this case,

312
00:08:34,133 --> 00:08:36,803
a normal index on email is sorted by the

313
00:08:36,803 --> 00:08:39,553
emails exactly as they were stored.

314
00:08:39,553 --> 00:08:42,443
The lowercase versions are not in that index.

315
00:08:42,443 --> 00:08:44,508
So the database can't use it here,

316
00:08:44,508 --> 00:08:46,773
unless you create an index on the same

317
00:08:46,773 --> 00:08:47,520
expression.

318
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:50,556
The second can also be adding a wildcard at

319
00:08:50,556 --> 00:08:52,291
the start of a LIKE.

320
00:08:52,291 --> 00:08:55,070
The index is sorted by how each value starts.

321
00:08:55,070 --> 00:08:57,090
And if you don't know how it starts,

322
00:08:57,090 --> 00:08:59,130
the index cannot help you find it.

323
00:08:59,130 --> 00:09:01,810
Another database index gotcha is

324
00:09:01,810 --> 00:09:03,160
that in MySQL,

325
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:04,490
when you add a foreign key,

326
00:09:04,490 --> 00:09:07,240
the database creates an index on that column

327
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:08,175
for you automatically,

328
00:09:08,175 --> 00:09:10,056
if there isn't one already.

329
00:09:10,056 --> 00:09:10,748
in PostgreSQL,

330
00:09:10,748 --> 00:09:11,700
it doesn't.

331
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:13,250
So if you're on Postgres,

332
00:09:13,250 --> 00:09:15,080
or you've moved from MySQL to Postgres,

333
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:17,060
you have to check and confirm that your

334
00:09:17,060 --> 00:09:18,010
foreign key

335
00:09:18,010 --> 00:09:19,650
columns actually have indexes.

336
00:09:19,650 --> 00:09:22,132
And it's one of the common reasons why joins

337
00:09:22,132 --> 00:09:23,660
suddenly get slow.

338
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:24,080
So again,

339
00:09:24,080 --> 00:09:26,330
to reiterate when to actually use a database

340
00:09:26,330 --> 00:09:28,287
index, use it when

341
00:09:28,287 --> 00:09:31,023
a column keeps showing up in your WHERE

342
00:09:31,023 --> 00:09:33,097
clauses, your joins or your ORDER BY.

343
00:09:33,097 --> 00:09:35,557
So say you are sorting a million orders by

344
00:09:35,557 --> 00:09:37,450
date, and you only show the first 10.

345
00:09:37,450 --> 00:09:38,450
If there's no index,

346
00:09:38,450 --> 00:09:41,430
the database has to look at a million rows

347
00:09:41,430 --> 00:09:44,680
and sort them before it can give you those

348
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:45,520
10.

349
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:46,250
But with an index,

350
00:09:46,250 --> 00:09:48,317
it reads them in order and stops after the

351
00:09:48,317 --> 00:09:49,073
first 10.

352
00:09:49,073 --> 00:09:50,713
Also, when you use EXPLAIN,

353
00:09:50,713 --> 00:09:53,097
and you realize that a query doing a full

354
00:09:53,097 --> 00:09:54,067
table scan,

355
00:09:54,067 --> 00:09:55,137
and it is slow,

356
00:09:55,137 --> 00:09:57,787
then maybe you need an index there.

357
00:09:57,787 --> 00:09:59,017
So if your query is slow,

358
00:09:59,017 --> 00:10:00,587
you probably consider

359
00:10:00,587 --> 00:10:02,007
adding an index

360
00:10:02,007 --> 00:10:03,867
there. And if it's doing a full scan.

361
00:10:03,867 --> 00:10:04,437
Also,

362
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:06,557
when a table gets more read,

363
00:10:06,557 --> 00:10:09,047
like you read it a lot more than you

364
00:10:09,047 --> 00:10:09,587
write it,

365
00:10:09,587 --> 00:10:11,237
that's also a good situation for a database

366
00:10:11,237 --> 00:10:12,897
index. But you shouldn't do that when the

367
00:10:12,897 --> 00:10:13,707
table is small.

368
00:10:13,707 --> 00:10:15,624
Or when table is constantly written to and

369
00:10:15,624 --> 00:10:16,367
rarely searched,

370
00:10:16,367 --> 00:10:18,507
then you probably don't need a database index

371
00:10:18,507 --> 00:10:19,577
in that situation.

372
00:10:19,577 --> 00:10:21,537
So that's it with database indexes.

373
00:10:21,537 --> 00:10:22,497
Bye.
